 36CA c  1977Tr03: First report on {+36}Ca nuclide from mass excess in reaction:
 36CA2c  {+40}Ca({+4}He,{+8}He), deduced mass excess.                           
 36CA c  1981Ay01: First identification of this nuclide in reaction:            
 36CA2c  {+40}Ca({+3}He,|a3n) at 95 MeV. Measured delayed proton decay and      
 36CA3c  isotopic half-life; Berkeley cyclotron facility and recoil-atom mass   
 36CA4c  analyzer.                                                              
 36CA c  1995Ga16: {+36}Ca produced using a radioactive ion beam from ISOLDE    
 36CA2c  online isotope separator with a Ti target at CERN, measured delayed    
 36CA2c  proton decay of {+36}Ca.                                               
 36CA c  1997Tr05, 1995Tr02: {+36}Ca produced by fragmentation of {+40}Ca beam  
 36CA2c  with a {+9}Be target at 300 MeV/nucleon beam energy, fragments         
 36CA3c  separated using FRS facility at GSI. Measured delayed proton branches  
 36CA4c  and isotopic half-life.                                                
 36CA c  2001Lo11: {+36}Ca produced by fragmentation of {+40}Ca beam at 95 MeV/ 
 36CA2c  nucleon with Ni target, fragments separated with LISE3 spectrometer    
 36CA3c  at GANIL facility. Measured delayed proton branches.                   
 36CA c  2007Do11, 2007Bu15: {+9}Be({+37}Ca,X|g) one-neutron knockout           
 36CA2c  reaction (GSA and GANIL), identification of first 2+ state             
 36CA c  {+36}Sc nuclide which can possibly decay by |b to {+36}Ca is not       
 36CA2c  yet identified                                                         
 36CA c  {+37}Sc nuclide which can possibly decay by protons to {+36}Ca is not  
 36CA2c  yet identified                                                         
 36CA c  {+37}Ti nuclide which can possibly decay by |b-delayed protons to      
 36CA2c  {+36}Ca is not yet identified                                          
 36CA c  {+38}Ti nuclide which will most likely decay by two-proton emission    
 36CA2c  to {+36}Ca is not yet identified but its search and upper limit        
 36CA3c  of 120 ns for half-life has been reported by 1996Bl21                  
